```

**Every compute register is zero.** The CP consumed 64 of 75 dwords on ring 1 and then
**stalled** on one of the SET_SH_REG packets. It never reached the IB/dispatch. This is why:
- Previous runs showed `Dispatch rc=0` — that was because the probe's mid-dispatch
  commit+wait exposed the stall that was previously masked by the full-batch commit.
  Without the probe, the CP would eventually time out on WaitFenceVRAM, but the fence
  on some runs DID fire (the CP consumed past the stall point on full-batch runs where
  the ring wrapped or the IB path didn't block).
- All previous `dst=0` results: the wavefront never launched because the compute state was
  never programmed — all registers stayed at zero.

**The SET_SH_REG packets with `SHADER_TYPE_COMPUTE` (bit 1 of PM4 header) are not supported on
SI ring 1 (MEC).** On Southern Islands, rings 1/2 are handled by the MEC (Micro Engine Compute),
which has a LIMITED packet set compared to the ME (ring 0). The Linux kernel `radeon` driver
(si.c / `si_ring_test_compute()`) only does compute dispatch on ring 0 (the GFX ring) via
`radeon_ring_write()` to ring 0. Mesa/Clover also emit compute on the GFX ring on SI.

GCN1 (SI) does NOT have a true async compute engine — that was added in CIK (GCN2, Sea Islands)
with the MEC getting full SET_SH_REG support. On SI, the "compute rings" (1/2) are primarily
for CP internal scheduling, not for user compute dispatch with full register programming.

